<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Entitlements for the hardened runtime, required once the app is signed
  with a Developer ID certificate. Notarization refuses a build without
  the hardened runtime, and the hardened runtime blocks each of the
  things below unless they are declared.

  These were read out of the runtime's own shipped code signature rather
  than guessed (Hammerspoon 1.1.1 is itself notarized, so its set is
  known-good). Its privacy entitlements for camera, microphone,
  contacts, calendars, location and photos are dropped here: this app
  touches none of them, and asking for them would show up in the
  consent prompts users see.

  Removing any of the remaining four breaks the app in a way that will
  not appear until it runs on a machine other than the build runner:

  automation.apple-events
      Every Excel action goes through AppleScript (hs.osascript).
      Without this, sending an Apple Event fails outright — the whole
      product stops working.

  cs.disable-library-validation
      The engine loads its Lua extension modules as separate .so files
      from Contents/Frameworks/hs. Library validation only permits
      loading code signed by the same team, and these are signed by
      whoever built the runtime.

  cs.allow-jit, cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      The runtime allocates and executes memory at run time. Kept
      because it ships with them; a crash from removing them would only
      surface under specific workloads, which is not a good trade for
      tidiness.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
